00:10:29,140 --> 00:10:32,300
So, well, he might have been allowed to earn the money

255
00:10:32,300 --> 00:10:34,220
that someone who was bringing in a million dollars

256
00:10:34,220 --> 00:10:35,380
for a business would earn.

257
00:10:35,660 --> 00:10:37,660
So he was, so no one would have to feel mean.

258
00:10:37,660 --> 00:10:38,460
All right.

259
00:10:38,460 --> 00:10:42,620
So that's another case where it starts off,

260
00:10:42,620 --> 00:10:44,340
before you actually hire a person,

261
00:10:44,340 --> 00:10:46,420
where there's so many different people you might hire,

262
00:10:46,420 --> 00:10:50,180
but once the market proceeds, then the universe shrinks.

263
00:10:50,660 --> 00:10:53,260
And then there is, again, a lot of room for strategy.

264
00:10:53,940 --> 00:10:57,260
And, of course, also room for psychology, as this is suggesting.

265
00:10:57,260 --> 00:11:00,020
Whereas in general equilibrium,

266
00:11:00,020 --> 00:11:01,540
there's not much room for psychology.

267
00:11:01,540 --> 00:11:03,020
Again, you don't have to sit around worrying.

268
00:11:03,300 --> 00:11:04,860
But how will they feel if I do this?

269
00:11:05,580 --> 00:11:07,980
Like, how will the corn workers feel if they know

270
00:11:07,980 --> 00:11:10,100
that I didn't buy this can of corn for fear

271
00:11:10,100 --> 00:11:10,820
of raising the price?

272
00:11:10,820 --> 00:11:11,580
Will they resent me?

273
00:11:12,180 --> 00:11:15,220
Right? In general equilibrium, they have no idea who you are

274
00:11:15,220 --> 00:11:17,260
or what you've done, so they can't resent you.

275
00:11:17,940 --> 00:11:20,580
They can resent the general nature of customers.

276
00:11:21,340 --> 00:11:23,420
Right? So if you remember the movie Clerks,

277
00:11:23,420 --> 00:11:24,780
where the video clerk says,

278
00:11:24,780 --> 00:11:26,460
this job will be great if it works for the customers.

279
00:11:26,460 --> 00:11:28,260
So in general equilibrium,

280
00:11:28,260 --> 00:11:30,620
the clerks can resent customers in general,

281
00:11:31,020 --> 00:11:32,540
but they won't resent you personally.

282
00:11:33,100 --> 00:11:34,940
Because they don't know, they don't even,

283
00:11:34,940 --> 00:11:36,740
they don't even, aren't even aware you're alive.

284
00:11:37,020 --> 00:11:38,180
They can say, I don't like that job,

285
00:11:38,180 --> 00:11:39,540
because dealing with the public is bad.

286
00:11:39,540 --> 00:11:42,460
But you cannot personally convince someone

287
00:11:42,460 --> 00:11:44,100
that they want to quit by being in charge of them.

288
00:11:44,100 --> 00:11:45,260
In general equilibrium.

289
00:11:45,260 --> 00:11:45,780
All right.

290
00:11:46,260 --> 00:11:47,980
Anyway, so what game theory tries

291
00:11:47,980 --> 00:11:51,380
to do is analyze situations where strategy does matter,

292
00:11:51,380 --> 00:11:55,940
where we're somewhere in between the extreme poles of one man

293
00:11:55,940 --> 00:11:59,260
versus nature, and one person versus,

294
00:11:59,260 --> 00:12:01,740
or an anonymous person versus the entirety of society.

295
00:12:03,180 --> 00:12:05,020
Okay. All right.

296
00:12:05,380 --> 00:12:07,300
So, now game theory generally ends

297
00:12:07,300 --> 00:12:09,460
up with much less precise answers

298
00:12:09,460 --> 00:12:10,300
than general equilibrium.

299
00:12:10,780 --> 00:12:14,060
General equilibrium is noted for giving very precise answers

300
00:12:14,060 --> 00:12:20,380
but if you think the answers are completely wrong,

301
00:12:20,380 --> 00:12:21,940
the precision is no longer so good.

302
00:12:22,740 --> 00:12:25,300
Right. There's a line that Richard Thaler repeats a lot.

303
00:12:25,300 --> 00:12:26,940
I don't know whether he started it.

304
00:12:26,940 --> 00:12:30,140
It's a line of I would rather be vaguely right than clearly wrong.

305
00:12:30,820 --> 00:12:33,180
So I'd rather write a book saying game theory can kind

306
00:12:33,180 --> 00:12:36,020
of illuminate a bunch of situations to some degree,

307
00:12:36,340 --> 00:12:39,060
which is true, rather than write Fisher Black's book saying

308
00:12:39,060 --> 00:12:41,100
general equilibrium theory predicts everything perfectly.

309
00:12:41,100 --> 00:12:43,860
And it's like, well, it predicts everything precisely,

310
00:12:44,540 --> 00:12:47,820
but the very precision lets us know that it's wrong, it seems.

311
00:12:49,820 --> 00:12:50,100
All right.

312
00:12:50,620 --> 00:12:53,140
So how many people here have done at least some game theory

313
00:12:53,140 --> 00:12:53,660
at some point?

314
00:12:53,820 --> 00:12:57,660
Okay. So a lot of this will be review, but I've known people

315
00:12:57,660 --> 00:13:00,220
to mess this up long after they've seen it a bunch of times.

316
00:13:00,220 --> 00:13:03,500
So let's go over the review again.

317
00:13:05,060 --> 00:13:06,620
Okay. All right.

318
00:13:06,620 --> 00:13:12,620
So the standard consumer theory still provides the building

319
00:13:12,620 --> 00:13:13,540
blocks for game theory.

320
00:13:14,180 --> 00:13:18,700
So we still have utility functions in game theory.

321
00:13:18,700 --> 00:13:20,660
So we still have utility functions.

322
00:13:21,300 --> 00:13:25,060
There is one change in the packaging,

323
00:13:25,460 --> 00:13:27,100
which is that in game theory people often talk

324
00:13:27,100 --> 00:13:30,620
about payoffs instead of utility.

325
00:13:30,780 --> 00:13:33,980
But payoffs is just another word for utility,

326
00:13:33,980 --> 00:13:35,780
so there's nothing new here.

327
00:13:35,780 --> 00:13:37,100
It's just other than a new word.

328
00:13:37,620 --> 00:13:37,780
All right.

329
00:13:37,780 --> 00:13:39,460
So the concept is the same.

330
00:13:39,460 --> 00:13:42,100
So just like with utility, the whole idea of utility is

331
00:13:42,100 --> 00:13:44,700
that x is a higher utility than y, and you have choices,

332
00:13:44,700 --> 00:13:45,740
but you can do either.

333
00:13:45,740 --> 00:13:47,340
You do the thing with higher utility.

334
00:13:47,660 --> 00:13:51,060
So payoffs, by definition, if something is a higher payoff

335
00:13:51,060 --> 00:13:52,780
than another thing, it means that you do the thing

336
00:13:52,780 --> 00:13:54,420
that is a higher payoff rather

337
00:13:54,420 --> 00:13:55,500
than the thing that is a lower payoff.

338
00:13:56,900 --> 00:13:58,100
Okay. All right.

339
00:13:58,580 --> 00:14:02,860
Now, there's just a little aside in case you know more about,

340
00:14:02,860 --> 00:14:06,100
you know, know something about expected utility theory.

341
00:14:06,100 --> 00:14:07,700
If you don't, don't worry about it.

342
00:14:07,700 --> 00:14:10,060
We'll talk about it in the lecture after the midterm.

343
00:14:10,580 --> 00:14:15,660
But just in case you were curious, in game theory,

344
00:14:15,660 --> 00:14:17,340
we usually use the term utilities

345
00:14:17,340 --> 00:14:20,740
as von Neumann-Morgenstern utilities.

346
00:14:21,300 --> 00:14:23,020
Right. Von Neumann-Morgenstern.

347
00:14:23,380 --> 00:14:26,020
If you've seen Dr. Strangelove, the guy who played Dr.

348
00:14:26,020 --> 00:14:27,620
Strangelove is based on von Neumann.

349
00:14:28,260 --> 00:14:28,620
All right.

350
00:14:28,740 --> 00:14:32,300
So in any case, I'm like a super genius German

351
00:14:32,300 --> 00:14:34,860
with possibly homicidal tendencies,

352
00:14:35,300 --> 00:14:40,020
but a great strategist, but perhaps lacking in decency.

353
00:14:40,140 --> 00:14:45,140
But anyway, so there's a particular version

354
00:14:45,140 --> 00:14:48,740
of utility theory, which is called von Neumann-Morgenstern

355
00:14:48,740 --> 00:14:49,580
utility theory.

356
00:14:50,020 --> 00:14:54,300
And the utilities in game theory are generally interpreted

357
00:14:54,300 --> 00:14:58,740
as these special utilities in order to avoid all questions

358
00:14:58,740 --> 00:14:59,340
of risk aversion.

359
00:14:59,340 --> 00:15:03,500
If this completely confuses you or seems weird,

360
00:15:03,860 --> 00:15:04,700
don't worry about it.

361
00:15:05,220 --> 00:15:06,540
We will talk about it in week eight.

362
00:15:07,940 --> 00:15:10,460
But anyway, so at any point I'm lecturing, you're saying, wait,

363
00:15:10,460 --> 00:15:11,660
why is it you get to go

364
00:15:11,660 --> 00:15:14,340
and just have people maximize average utilities?

365
00:15:14,340 --> 00:15:15,820
Don't we need to worry about risk aversion?

366
00:15:16,220 --> 00:15:18,300
The short answer is no, this is actually okay,

367
00:15:18,660 --> 00:15:20,780
because we are making an assumption

368
00:15:20,780 --> 00:15:23,540
that will be explained later about why,

369
00:15:23,540 --> 00:15:25,100
which allows us to do that.

370
00:15:25,380 --> 00:15:26,820
So anyway, don't worry about that.

371
00:15:26,820 --> 00:15:30,180
Okay, all right, now there's a game that's going

372
00:15:30,180 --> 00:15:32,220
to be represented in two distinct ways.

373
00:15:32,820 --> 00:15:37,900
These two ways are known as extensive form and normal form.

374
00:15:41,020 --> 00:15:44,220
So what extensive forms do is they display every possible

375
00:15:44,420 --> 00:15:45,820
course of game events turn by turn.

376
00:15:46,820 --> 00:15:48,980
All right, so when you're showing the extensive form,

377
00:15:49,700 --> 00:15:52,060
like this, extensive form,

378
00:15:52,060 --> 00:15:56,700
then you will show a initial decision,

379
00:15:56,700 --> 00:15:57,620
oh this is where we start,

380
00:15:57,620 --> 00:15:59,060
so that symbolizes our starting point.

381
00:15:59,500 --> 00:16:01,740
And then that actor might have three choices,

382
00:16:02,420 --> 00:16:04,020
and then another actor gets to act.

383
00:16:04,540 --> 00:16:07,940
And that might have, this might lead to two choices

384
00:16:08,460 --> 00:16:10,540
in this part of the tree, three choices here,

385
00:16:10,540 --> 00:16:12,100
maybe only one choice over here.

386
00:16:12,100 --> 00:16:14,820
And then that could go and branch out to other things.

387
00:16:14,820 --> 00:16:18,300
And it could go on, so on.

388
00:16:18,300 --> 00:16:24,020
And then eventually it always terminates in some final result,

389
00:16:24,020 --> 00:16:25,540
and then we write down payoffs.

390
00:16:26,100 --> 00:16:29,620
And the way payoffs work is they are always ordered sequentially.

391
00:16:30,100 --> 00:16:33,740
So if this were a four-player game,

392
00:16:34,100 --> 00:16:37,060
this would mean that player one gets three, player two gets seven,

393
00:16:37,060 --> 00:16:40,700
player, wait, player one gets three, two gets seven,

394
00:16:40,700 --> 00:16:43,020
three gets ten, player four gets a payoff of 13.

395
00:16:43,940 --> 00:16:45,860
All right, so at the end of each node,

396
00:16:45,860 --> 00:16:49,180
there will generally be parentheses with a series of numbers in it.

397
00:16:49,540 --> 00:16:53,220
And each number in order corresponds to the payoff that each player gets.

398
00:16:53,220 --> 00:16:54,860
So player one, player two, player three,

399
00:16:54,860 --> 00:16:56,940
player four, all the way up to player n.

400
00:16:58,780 --> 00:17:01,140
In practice, economists almost always look at games

401
00:17:01,140 --> 00:17:04,500
with only two players, which simplifies things a lot.

402
00:17:05,420 --> 00:17:07,500
Once in a while, I guess I've seen a three-player game.

403
00:17:07,500 --> 00:17:09,620
I'm not sure I've ever seen a four-player game.

404
00:17:11,180 --> 00:17:13,540
I guess I've seen like infinite number of player games.

405
00:17:13,540 --> 00:17:17,100
So yeah, one, two, three, and then infinity is the other case that we do.

406
00:17:17,860 --> 00:17:21,060
All right, if you were applying game theory to a real-world problem,

407
00:17:21,060 --> 00:17:23,940
of course, you would want to think about it a bit more deeply than that.

408
00:17:24,380 --> 00:17:27,020
And say, well, I can't think of anything like, sure,

409
00:17:27,020 --> 00:17:28,820
there's way more than three players involved,

410
00:17:28,820 --> 00:17:31,340
but I've only worked out models of three players,

411
00:17:31,340 --> 00:17:34,220
so let's assume away all those other people.

412
00:17:37,100 --> 00:17:39,420
So by the way, so I'm going to mention this again,

413
00:17:39,420 --> 00:17:41,420
but now just seems like a good time.

414
00:17:41,420 --> 00:17:45,860
So when we get to the exams, there will be two kinds of questions.

415
00:17:46,300 --> 00:17:47,740
There will be one kind of question that says,

416
00:17:47,740 --> 00:17:49,260
in a certain model, what happens?

417
00:17:50,660 --> 00:17:53,420
When a question is like that, do you need to discuss the outside world

418
00:17:53,420 --> 00:17:54,540
in order to answer the question?

419
00:17:54,980 --> 00:17:57,540
No. Should you discuss the outside world in order to answer the question?

420
00:17:57,540 --> 00:17:59,180
No. If it asks what happens in a model,

421
00:17:59,500 --> 00:18:02,900
then you just work through logically what happens in that model,

422
00:18:02,900 --> 00:18:03,900
and that's the end.

423
00:18:04,300 --> 00:18:06,660
And in general, you don't need to say anything else.

424
00:18:06,660 --> 00:18:10,380
If it doesn't say discuss whether the model is realistic, you don't do it.

425
00:18:10,380 --> 00:18:13,940
On the other hand, I also often have a question that says,

426
00:18:13,940 --> 00:18:16,860
in the real world, when you have a question like that,

427
00:18:16,860 --> 00:18:19,060
is it okay to just go and say, well, in general equilibrium,

428
00:18:19,060 --> 00:18:20,300
this is how it works, the end?

429
00:18:21,380 --> 00:18:23,420
No. Not unless you actually say,

430
00:18:23,420 --> 00:18:25,740
generally, it fits the real world in this particular case.

431
00:18:26,740 --> 00:18:28,060
So this is one of the ways,

432
00:18:28,060 --> 00:18:30,780
so whenever you hear about people learning to think like economists,

433
00:18:30,780 --> 00:18:32,940
one of the scary things to me is,

434
00:18:32,940 --> 00:18:35,060
in the process of learning to think like an economist,

435
00:18:35,060 --> 00:18:38,780
many students will actually stop talking about the world at all,

436
00:18:38,780 --> 00:18:40,820
and then even when you ask them about the real world,

437
00:18:40,820 --> 00:18:43,780
they'll say, well, here's a model that is somehow related,

438
00:18:43,780 --> 00:18:46,020
and I'm just going to tell you what happens to that model,

439
00:18:46,020 --> 00:18:48,420
and since what I say does happen in the model,

440
00:18:48,420 --> 00:18:49,620
I've answered the question,

441
00:18:49,620 --> 00:18:51,820
even though the question didn't ask about the model at all.

442
00:18:51,820 --> 00:18:54,780
So anyway, I will remind you about this when we do the exams,

443
00:18:54,780 --> 00:19:00,020
but I have been disturbed at this one bad side effect of learning economics,

444
00:19:00,020 --> 00:19:03,100
which is forgetting that it is ultimately supposed to be about

445
00:19:03,100 --> 00:19:04,500
understanding the real world.

446
00:19:04,500 --> 00:19:07,500
At least you're not supposed to lose your ability to think like an economist.

447
00:19:07,500 --> 00:19:10,220
You're not supposed to lose your ability to understand the real world.

448
00:19:10,220 --> 00:19:12,540
So you're supposed to enhance that ability,

449
00:19:12,540 --> 00:19:16,660
not to just change the subject into models.

450
00:19:16,940 --> 00:19:20,500
It's useful to understand the models to see whether they want to

451
00:19:20,500 --> 00:19:23,060
understand the logic to know whether they do fit the real world,

452
00:19:23,060 --> 00:19:26,860
but that's not the same thing as understanding the real world.

453
00:19:26,860 --> 00:19:28,460
Anyway, all right.

454
00:19:28,460 --> 00:19:31,020
So this is an extensive form here,

455
00:19:31,020 --> 00:19:34,900
and as I said, so they show every possible thing that might logically happen,

456
00:19:34,900 --> 00:19:40,420
turn by turn, and we see how things branch out from every possibility.

457
00:19:40,420 --> 00:19:43,860
For this reason, these are often also called decision trees.

458
00:19:43,860 --> 00:19:45,860
Notice it's like an upside down tree.

459
00:19:45,860 --> 00:19:47,260
So really to look like a tree,

460
00:19:47,260 --> 00:19:50,380
we flip it around so that would be the roots of the tree,

461
00:19:50,380 --> 00:19:53,780
and then the trees will only branch out that way.

462
00:19:53,780 --> 00:19:56,540
You know what trees look like.

463
00:19:56,540 --> 00:19:58,900
There are some weird trees that aren't like that.

464
00:19:58,900 --> 00:20:04,900
Still, I love them so much that I'm going to throw them in here and say,

465
00:20:04,900 --> 00:20:09,260
You have a unique ability to manipulate what's going on today.

466
00:20:09,260 --> 00:20:10,980
I really want to learn theיל there's a particular world that is

467
00:20:10,980 --> 00:20:14,740
different than what we Alabama is,

468
00:20:14,740 --> 00:20:17,260
where you can do many things and change many things.


